WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.148 --> 00:00:05.172
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday. 
It's the last week of Pizzamas

00:00:05.213 --> 00:00:08.550
That means there's only a few more days to get
your Pizzamas stuff at Pizzamas.com

00:00:08.591 --> 00:00:13.512
 including all of our amazing t-shirts and this
super snuggly blanket and this color changing mug!

00:00:13.554 --> 00:00:16.640
By the way, we now have some discount bundles 
including the cozy bundle 

00:00:16.682 --> 00:00:19.726
which includes this blanket and 
the mug for $30 off

00:00:19.768 --> 00:00:22.437
Okay Hank so full disclosure 
I'm anxious so

00:00:22.479 --> 00:00:28.123
let's go outside because it does actually help me to like
touch grass and be amid trees

00:00:28.504 --> 00:00:31.386
It's so weird to me how much
life there is on earth like

00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:32.612
This is life

00:00:32.877 --> 00:00:33.989
This is life

00:00:34.406 --> 00:00:35.865
This is life

00:00:35.865 --> 00:00:38.493
You made a TikTok once where you pointed
out that humans have

00:00:38.573 --> 00:00:42.580
relatively brief lifespans and
our species hasn't been around for that long

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.519
But if we think
of ourselves as part of one

00:00:45.639 --> 00:00:49.085
continuous biological
phenomenon called life

00:00:49.127 --> 00:00:51.944
then things start to look quite
different because life on

00:00:51.963 --> 00:00:57.843
Earth has existed continuously
for about 3.7 billion years

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:02.472
Which is around 23% of the
history of the entire universe

00:01:02.514 --> 00:01:05.124
 So like, we are young, and most species we

00:01:05.184 --> 00:01:07.602
currently share the planet
with are fairly young

00:01:07.643 --> 00:01:10.807
Like, even the tuatara, which
existed before the emergence

00:01:10.847 --> 00:01:14.650
of large dinosaurs, is only
around 250 million years old,

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.944
which is less than 10% of the
history of life on Earth

00:01:17.986 --> 00:01:22.135
So yeah, we're new, most other
things that we see are new,

00:01:22.595 --> 00:01:25.534
But life is very old

00:01:25.534 --> 00:01:27.318
And I do find that a little comforting.

00:01:27.318 --> 00:01:29.459
But when you're walking
through an urban-ish forest,

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:31.915
you don't only see life, of course

00:01:31.956 --> 00:01:35.376
You also see steel husks and concrete structures

00:01:35.418 --> 00:01:39.463
that were once used to house barrels 
of whiskey during Prohibition 

00:01:39.505 --> 00:01:43.591
This forest used to be private property 
owned by Albert Lieber 

00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:46.344
who made and lost a fortune selling booze

00:01:46.386 --> 00:01:50.598
and who was also the grandfather 
of the novelist Kurt Vonnegut 

00:01:50.598 --> 00:01:54.017
And we're in a city, so the human-shaped
world is everywhere 

00:01:54.059 --> 00:01:59.814
Even in the forest, from the invasive
ivy to the extraordinarily polluted White River

00:01:59.856 --> 00:02:04.026
There's no full separation between
these worlds, not really

00:02:04.068 --> 00:02:06.028
We try to claim that this is artificial

00:02:06.028 --> 00:02:07.529
 and this is natural 

00:02:07.571 --> 00:02:11.930
but concrete and steel are made out of earth,
just like everything else is

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:14.077
Alright, I'm starting to
breathe a little easier

00:02:14.118 --> 00:02:17.538
I'm part of a continuous process
of life that stretches back 

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.124
A quarter of the way to the
beginning of time

00:02:20.165 --> 00:02:21.583
and I'm in a world

00:02:21.625 --> 00:02:22.918
I'm walking

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:25.253
This is a good place to be, all things considered

00:02:25.295 --> 00:02:26.921
It's a good earth

00:02:26.963 --> 00:02:30.091
My friend Henry in Sierra Leone 
likes to say that when he sees

00:02:30.132 --> 00:02:33.844
beauty or goodness in the world 
that he is encouraged 

00:02:33.886 --> 00:02:36.179
So yeah, I'm feeling encouraged

00:02:36.221 --> 00:02:38.598
One nice thing about 
Indianapolis in October

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.475
the leaves are starting to fall

00:02:40.516 --> 00:02:43.352
If you pitched this idea to someone
who'd never been to Earth

00:02:43.394 --> 00:02:46.355
I think they would find it
phenomenally beautiful

00:02:46.397 --> 00:02:49.358
So each cycle, 
we would explain to these non-Earth folk,

00:02:49.399 --> 00:02:54.445
Each cycle, it gets colder in parts
of our world as we tilt away from our star

00:02:54.487 --> 00:02:58.032
and our most numerous 
and visually prominent  life forms, 

00:02:58.074 --> 00:03:01.910
which are called trees, 
simultaneously change

00:03:01.952 --> 00:03:07.614
 The fluttering soft green appendages at the end 
of these trees are called leaves and

00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:12.503
these leaves erupt into bright
colors that we associate with our star

00:03:12.545 --> 00:03:16.280
Reds, oranges,
yellows and then huge swaths

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.510
of leaves fall to the ground
until the ground is covered with them

00:03:20.552 --> 00:03:23.513
The trees spend the cold months 
without their leaves 

00:03:23.554 --> 00:03:27.349
and then, as our earth begins to 
tilt back toward its star

00:03:27.391 --> 00:03:29.768
the trees regrow their leaves

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:33.305
This goes on growing and falling, 
growing and falling

00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:36.232
for dozens or even hundreds of cycles

00:03:36.274 --> 00:03:41.111
It goes on for so long that leaves 
dropped by young trees may in time 

00:03:41.111 --> 00:03:45.740
become soil that helps support 
that same tree as it gets older

00:03:45.782 --> 00:03:50.578
In a very real way, our trees are nourished
by what they let go of

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.164
by what they leave behind

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:56.208
What an encouragement I find that to be

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.503
Hank, I'll see you tomorrow.

